(The whole-seconds form must report whole truncated seconds of the real
 elapsed time, not the calendar second fields it used to subtract. A 1500
 millisecond wait is one and a half seconds, which truncates to one; a 100
 millisecond wait is a tenth of a second, which truncates to zero. Both
 are now exact, because the macro divides the full nanosecond total by
 NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND instead of subtracting second fields, so neither
 depends on where the interval fell within its starting second. The old
 form read one for a 100 millisecond wait that straddled a boundary - a
 tenfold error - and that case was uncovered. Asserting equality, not a
 range, also guards the unit split: if `in seconds` ever returned
 milliseconds it would read roughly 1500, not 1.)

a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 1500 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'seconds measured' is the clock's duration in seconds.
If 'seconds measured' is equal to 1 then,
    Print "fifteen hundred ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "fifteen hundred bad".
Start the clock.
Wait 100 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'subsecond seconds' is the clock's duration in seconds.
If 'subsecond seconds' is equal to 0 then,
    Print "hundred ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "hundred bad".